WEBVTT
Kind: captions

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:05.220 position:63%
MATT: This is the Big Thunder Mountain Railroad in the Magic Kingdom here at Disney World.

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:08.570 position:63%
And while it is a very old ride,
it was very recently refurbished,

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:12.850 position:63%
and everyone now describes the
ride as being much smoother.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:18.040 position:63%
And they're right, because there's been
mathematical advances in the last 50

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:22.720 position:63%
years, and I got to speak to the principal
ride project engineer who did the

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:25.720 position:63%
MATT: calculations to improve
the maths of this ride.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:33.440 position:63%
[upbeat music]

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:38.280 position:63%
Yes, while at Disney World, I got to
meet up with Imagineer Nick Roedl,

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.580 position:63%
who worked on Thunder Mountain, and not
only were they ready to talk some

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:43.910 position:63%
MATT: mathematics, but check
out what they're carrying.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:48.900 position:63%
That's actual design diagrams
for rides at Disney World,

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:52.360 position:63%
some of which we can and some of
which we can't show you in this video.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:56.520 position:63%
And yes, later I get to go on Big Thunder
Railroad with Nick and the diagrams,

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:00.810 position:63%
but for now, I asked him what it takes
to design a good ride.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.510 position:63%
NICK: So there's a lot of factors
that go into ride quality.

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:06.270 position:63%
You know, what is it that
the guests are going to feel?

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:10.630 position:63%
Then that'll influence how fast we
wanna go, how quickly we wanna turn,

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:14.140 position:63%
because it all comes down
to what kind of G-forces,

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:17.350 position:63%
what kind of accelerations we're imparting
on that person while they're riding.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:19.230 position:63%
MATT: Now, the PeopleMover
is a special case.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:20.140 position:63%
NICK: It is.

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:22.110 position:63%
MATT: This, when was this designed?

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:26.960 position:63%
NICK: This was designed in the early '70s, so we have documentation that dates back as far

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.130 position:63%
as 1970, '71.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:31.170 position:63%
It opened in 1975.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:33.730 position:63%
MATT: And how would this be
different to a modern ride?

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:38.190 position:63%
Well, so, if you were to compare it to,
like, a modern roller coaster,

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:43.500 position:63%
NICK: it would have a lot more consideration for the entry and exit into some of your

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:44.340 position:63%
turns,

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:46.410 position:63%
the way in which we're,

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:51.060 position:63%
engaged between the vehicle and
the wheels to prevent, you know,

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:52.960 position:63%
- Little oscillations, little bouncing-
- Yep.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:53.800 position:63%
NICK: Things like that.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:57.260 position:63%
But our manufacturing's
come a long way since then,

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:00.900 position:63%
so we have to look at it through the lens
of what they were doing back in the '70s

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:01.970 position:63%
when they were designing this.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:06.300 position:63%
MATT: So would this be constrained
both in terms of how it was designed

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:07.770 position:63%
and how it was manufactured?

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:11.380 position:63%
Yeah, I think it would be a combination
of the tools they had available when they

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:14.530 position:63%
NICK: were, you know, in the office actually sketching it out and designing it,

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:18.960 position:63%
with consideration for what was the
machine shop capable of at the time.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.160 position:63%
You know, what were the tools that they
actually had available to machine these

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:22.780 position:63%
parts?

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:24.850 position:63%
MATT: Yeah. How accurately
can you bend a pipe?

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:25.640 position:63%
- Truly.
- Yeah.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:29.110 position:63%
Absolutely. Today we can do that so
precisely,

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.180 position:63%
NICK: you know, in a way that they
couldn't have even dreamed of in the '70s.

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:34.410 position:63%
MATT: So I guess now you can
pick any curve and, say, make a

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:36.220 position:63%
NICK: Anything we want, infinitely
adjustable MATT: Or whatever matches this

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:36.940 position:63%
MATT: or whatever that matches this.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:38.190 position:63%
NICK: Infinitely adjustable.

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:41.310 position:63%
Whereas here they're like,
"Give us a bit of a circle."

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.380 position:63%
Yeah, absolutely. It's either
a straight line or it's part of a circle.

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:46.280 position:63%
- It's an arc.
- Right. Now, I'm very excited.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:47.080 position:63%
MATT: You're holding-

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:47.420 position:63%
NICK: Yes

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:47.960 position:63%
MATT: what is this?

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:53.800 position:63%
So this is a binder of the actual
drawings of the track here at the

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:55.470 position:63%
Tomorrowland Transit Authority
PeopleMover.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:55.840 position:63%
MATT: Yep.

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:57.860 position:63%
If there's any point where we need
to do maintenance on one of these rides,

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:59.340 position:63%
NICK: obviously we need to
know the way it was designed.

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:02.230 position:63%
Yep. And Nick was right.
As we looked through the diagrams,

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:07.300 position:63%
MATT: absolutely everything was made out of either a straight line or a section of a

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:07.800 position:63%
circle.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:10.350 position:63%
This is, you know, the next curve,
number 73.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:14.440 position:63%
Straight, 20-foot radius, straight.
Boom, boom, boom.

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:16.140 position:63%
- Wow.
- You know, no transition.

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:17.300 position:63%
- No.
- Nothing.

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:21.480 position:63%
Well, here's the thing.
These straights are tangent to these arcs.

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:22.300 position:63%
NICK: Mm-hmm.

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:25.180 position:63%
MATT: So your displacement, like,
your movement is continuous.

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:26.020 position:63%
NICK: Yes.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:32.040 position:63%
But there's no sideways acceleration
or force on a straight line.

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:32.440 position:63%
NICK: Nope.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:35.090 position:63%
And you're immediately into some
kind of centripetal...

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:36.600 position:63%
NICK: Yeah, yeah, V squared over R.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:37.720 position:63%
- Yeah, boom.
- It comes on immediately.

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:39.260 position:63%
- From zero to all of it.
- Yep.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:41.260 position:63%
- And then out to whatever is next.
- Yep.

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:43.820 position:63%
- So it's very discontinuous.
- Very abrupt.

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:44.930 position:63%
MATT: Yeah. So

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:47.370 position:63%
you've got your position is continuous.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:48.100 position:63%
NICK: Mm-hmm.

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:49.950 position:63%
MATT: Your speed is probably...

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:51.380 position:63%
- It is
- Continuous.

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:55.150 position:63%
NICK: For the most part, I actually have a
map here that shows the speed of the ride.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:56.890 position:63%
10 feet per second into a curve.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:57.420 position:63%
MATT: Yeah, you don't even know that.

00:03:57.460 --> 00:03:59.200 position:63%
NICK: Those lateral accelerations
come on immediately.

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:03.440 position:63%
MATT: And if they were careful, this, so, our first derivative of velocity could change

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:06.890 position:63%
continuously. They could slow you down
gradually and speed you back up again.

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:08.530 position:63%
MATT: I don't know what
they actually do with the...

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:14.000 position:63%
You could. So the way that the
PeopleMover moves the vehicles around...

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:14.340 position:63%
MATT: Yep

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:15.020 position:63%
NICK: there are,

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:18.790 position:63%
just under 600 electromagnetic stators,

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:20.240 position:63%
- Right
- coils in the track.

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:24.720 position:63%
NICK: And on the bottom of the vehicle is a large aluminium platen that just interacts

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:28.020 position:63%
with it, and when it comes in contact
or comes close to that magnet...

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:28.360 position:63%
MATT: Yep

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:31.510 position:63%
NICK: the eddy currents propel the
train in the direction that we want to.

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:37.260 position:63%
So, displacement continuous.
First derivative, pretty smooth.

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:37.780 position:63%
NICK: Yep, yep.

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:39.470 position:63%
MATT: But the moment you
get to acceleration...

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:40.320 position:63%
NICK: - Mm-hmm

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:41.850 position:63%
MATT: Discont... like, jerky.

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:43.320 position:63%
NICK: It's immediate. Yeah.

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:45.440 position:63%
What is it? The derivative
of acceleration is jerk.

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:46.260 position:63%
- Jerk, bingo.
- You're gonna feel that immediately.

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:48.280 position:63%
- Yeah, you feel that jerk.
- Yeah.

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:49.940 position:63%
MATT: And maybe a little
bit of snap. [laughs]

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:50.780 position:63%
NICK: Yeah, yeah.

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:52.940 position:63%
- Well, should we ride this?
- Absolutely.

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:53.740 position:63%
MATT: And feel the circles?

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:59.640 position:63%
Oh, and I think you'll feel it
in its elegance, too.

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:02.960 position:63%
NICK: I mean, it's not an
uncomfortable ride because it's,

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:04.410 position:63%
it's all very well controlled,

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:05.020 position:63%
MATT: Yeah

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:08.130 position:63%
NICK: but you will feel the
transitions or lack thereof.

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:09.900 position:63%
- Great. Let's do it.
- All right. Let's do it.

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:12.270 position:63%
MATT: So what radius
are we on at the moment?

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:14.540 position:63%
- Probably 109 foot.
- This is the 109 foot.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:15.450 position:63%
NICK: This is the 109.

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:19.900 position:63%
Actually, hold on. Let me just confirm.

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:20.740 position:63%
MATT: Yeah. We

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:22.000 position:63%
NICK: We just need to
consult really quickly.

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:23.020 position:63%
Well, why do you have to guess?
Yeah.

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:26.910 position:63%
Okay. Actually, no, I was wrong.
This is a 34-foot radius.

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:27.780 position:63%
MATT: Okay. Yeah.

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:28.960 position:63%
NICK: Yeah, 'cause we're on the inside.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:30.600 position:63%
