I mean, you know, we'll lay
it out, and if we see a spike,

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:39.270 position:63%
or some kind of acceleration that's too
high or what we would call a reversal,

00:09:39.310 --> 00:09:42.150 position:63%
which is too much one direction
and then too quickly the other,

00:09:42.190 --> 00:09:45.160 position:63%
and we'll fine-tune it until we
get those G-forces that we want.

00:09:45.250 --> 00:09:48.510 position:63%
Okay.
At last, it was time for Thunder Mountain.

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:51.760 position:63%
MATT: And interestingly, when
they were refurbishing it,

00:09:51.990 --> 00:09:57.850 position:63%
they wanted to fit the new track in the
same space around the rocks where the

00:09:57.890 --> 00:10:01.260 position:63%
old track had been. And as they were
trying to work out how to do that,

00:10:01.550 --> 00:10:03.660 position:63%
MATT: they made a rather
unusual discovery.

00:10:03.830 --> 00:10:07.310 position:63%
NICK: There's a point at the ride
where we found an interesting artefact,

00:10:07.350 --> 00:10:09.230 position:63%
- and we were looking to recreate it.
- Oh.

00:10:09.270 --> 00:10:12.700 position:63%
NICK: So throughout the ride, you know,
you've got all these series of curves.

00:10:13.610 --> 00:10:16.810 position:63%
After you go up the second lift hill,
that's the lift hill that's outside.

00:10:16.850 --> 00:10:17.190 position:63%
- Second lift hill.
- Yes.

00:10:17.230 --> 00:10:18.580 position:63%
- Yeah.
- And I'll point this out to you.

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:21.340 position:63%
NICK: You come around, you go
around this highlighted curve here,

00:10:21.870 --> 00:10:22.860 position:63%
and you go around this.

00:10:23.370 --> 00:10:27.560 position:63%
It's a 360 degree curve,
and then 180 degree curve.

00:10:27.870 --> 00:10:31.320 position:63%
We call it Spiral Butte, 'cause there's
a large butte going around in a spiral.

00:10:32.070 --> 00:10:33.880 position:63%
Now if you look at this picture here,

00:10:34.030 --> 00:10:36.580 position:63%
you'll see you've got a
perfect 360 degree curve,

00:10:36.850 --> 00:10:38.240 position:63%
and then you've got this 180 degree curve.

00:10:38.350 --> 00:10:41.570 position:63%
But do you see there where there's
that little straight piece right there?

00:10:41.610 --> 00:10:42.410 position:63%
MATT: Yes.

00:10:43.070 --> 00:10:44.610 position:63%
NICK: Right between the numbers 65 and 66.

00:10:44.650 --> 00:10:45.770 position:63%
MATT: Between 65 and 66.

00:10:45.810 --> 00:10:47.460 position:63%
NICK: So that was an artefact that

00:10:48.370 --> 00:10:51.400 position:63%
when we were surveying and
recreating the profile,

00:10:52.610 --> 00:10:54.010 position:63%
first, we said, "What is this?

00:10:54.050 --> 00:10:54.410 position:63%
Why is this here?"

00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:56.160 position:63%
MATT: Why? Why go straight?

00:10:56.270 --> 00:10:57.070 position:63%
NICK: Why would you go straight?

00:10:57.130 --> 00:10:59.430 position:63%
And it's an artefact of
the manufacturing process.

00:10:59.470 --> 00:11:01.580 position:63%
When they laid out the ride originally,

00:11:02.210 --> 00:11:04.910 position:63%
you know, they did do some, you know,

00:11:04.950 --> 00:11:06.800 position:63%
rudimentary computer-aided engineering.

00:11:07.330 --> 00:11:10.410 position:63%
But they were still limited
in how much they could render.

00:11:10.450 --> 00:11:14.110 position:63%
Our understanding was it couldn't
render more than 360 degrees.

00:11:14.150 --> 00:11:14.730 position:63%
So you can

00:11:14.770 --> 00:11:15.270 position:63%
MATT: Oh

00:11:15.310 --> 00:11:17.290 position:63%
NICK: this is that first 360 degrees.

00:11:17.330 --> 00:11:18.130 position:63%
So they plotted

00:11:18.270 --> 00:11:18.570 position:63%
MATT: Yeah

00:11:18.410 --> 00:11:20.610 position:63%
NICK: manufactured it. And then
right next to it on the right here,

00:11:20.650 --> 00:11:21.990 position:63%
that's the 180 degree turn.

00:11:22.030 --> 00:11:22.650 position:63%
MATT: That's the rest of it.

00:11:22.690 --> 00:11:26.250 position:63%
NICK: So they had to bring those two together, and the only way they could do it was with

00:11:26.290 --> 00:11:29.460 position:63%
a small sliver of straight
track in the field.

00:11:30.010 --> 00:11:30.810 position:63%
Now,

00:11:30.990 --> 00:11:31.750 position:63%
that's fine.

00:11:31.790 --> 00:11:32.980 position:63%
It's a mine coaster.

00:11:33.490 --> 00:11:36.250 position:63%
But we had to decide,
okay, what do we want to...

00:11:36.290 --> 00:11:39.720 position:63%
Like, we can't change the
ride profile to eliminate it.

00:11:40.830 --> 00:11:41.920 position:63%
Here's where it gets tricky.

00:11:42.250 --> 00:11:45.360 position:63%
When you start thinking about
banking and banking theory,

00:11:45.710 --> 00:11:46.010 position:63%
MATT: Yeah

00:11:45.770 --> 00:11:49.500 position:63%
NICK: you know, you bank the coaster
based on whether you want to have,

00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:52.360 position:63%
you know, positive G's, negative
G's, how much lateral G.

00:11:52.550 --> 00:11:55.050 position:63%
We consider Big Thunder
Mountain to be all underbanked.

00:11:55.090 --> 00:11:56.100 position:63%
So every

00:11:56.970 --> 00:11:57.270 position:63%
MATT: Yeah

00:11:57.050 --> 00:12:01.970 position:63%
NICK: we bank it, but we have less
banking than you would need for all your

00:12:02.010 --> 00:12:03.450 position:63%
G-forces to go straight down.

00:12:03.490 --> 00:12:03.790 position:63%
MATT: To go straight down.

00:12:03.570 --> 00:12:03.910 position:63%
NICK: So you get thrown up.

00:12:03.950 --> 00:12:04.980 position:63%
MATT: So you get a little bit of swing.

00:12:05.070 --> 00:12:05.550 position:63%
Okay.

00:12:05.590 --> 00:12:07.580 position:63%
NICK: So you're going
through that 360 degree turn.

00:12:07.670 --> 00:12:08.770 position:63%
You know, I'm banked like this.

00:12:08.810 --> 00:12:09.610 position:63%
MATT: Yep.

00:12:09.750 --> 00:12:11.280 position:63%
NICK: Then I hit that straight portion.

00:12:11.850 --> 00:12:15.880 position:63%
So we had to decide, do we wanna
just keep the banking consistent?

00:12:16.910 --> 00:12:20.230 position:63%
Or do we want to gradually come
out of it and go back into it?

00:12:20.270 --> 00:12:23.300 position:63%
Originally, it was banked just
consistently the whole time.

00:12:23.690 --> 00:12:28.340 position:63%
What we decided would actually be more
thrilling is to un-bank it and re-bank it.

00:12:28.410 --> 00:12:29.210 position:63%
MATT: Right.

00:12:29.330 --> 00:12:30.950 position:63%
NICK: Now, from the accelerometer...

00:12:30.990 --> 00:12:31.790 position:63%
MATT: Lean, if you will, to

00:12:32.110 --> 00:12:33.770 position:63%
NICK: Yeah. Now, from the
accelerometer perspective

00:12:33.810 --> 00:12:34.610 position:63%
MATT: Yeah

00:12:34.690 --> 00:12:35.980 position:63%
NICK: there's no change in the reading.

00:12:36.290 --> 00:12:40.120 position:63%
It's consistent because we're
relieving the banking angle

00:12:41.750 --> 00:12:42.110 position:63%
MATT: As you're losing

00:12:42.150 --> 00:12:43.310 position:63%
NICK: As it's starting to straighten out.

00:12:43.350 --> 00:12:44.050 position:63%
And then we re-bank

00:12:44.090 --> 00:12:45.320 position:63%
MATT: centrifugal. Right.

00:12:45.430 --> 00:12:50.080 position:63%
NICK: But the effect is that as a passenger, you actually get tossed in and out.

00:12:50.250 --> 00:12:54.050 position:63%
And so when we ride, I'm gonna point this
out because you'll feel your whole body

00:12:54.090 --> 00:12:55.740 position:63%
get tossed outward and inward.

00:12:56.490 --> 00:12:58.380 position:63%
We felt like that would be more thrilling.

00:12:58.450 --> 00:12:58.890 position:63%
MATT: Yeah.

00:12:58.930 --> 00:13:02.850 position:63%
NICK: Even though from an acceleration
standpoint your acceleration rate isn't

00:13:02.890 --> 00:13:05.930 position:63%
changing, we're actually
tossing you in and out, and it's

00:13:05.970 --> 00:13:08.860 position:63%
and it's all because of
this little artifact where

00:13:09.610 --> 00:13:14.640 position:63%
you know, we had to reconcile the fact
that there was this previously unknown

00:13:14.750 --> 00:13:18.030 position:63%
straight piece of track in the
middle of this 540-degree turn.

00:13:18.070 --> 00:13:21.150 position:63%
MATT: That's patching
together two ancient CAD files

00:13:21.190 --> 00:13:21.490 position:63%
NICK: Exactly

00:13:21.510 --> 00:13:22.310 position:63%
MATT: whatever they were doing.

00:13:22.390 --> 00:13:22.770 position:63%
NICK: Exactly.

00:13:22.810 --> 00:13:23.110 position:63%
MATT: Wow.

00:13:23.130 --> 00:13:25.180 position:63%
NICK: Yeah. So I'm gonna
point that out when we ride.

00:13:25.670 --> 00:13:26.570 position:63%
MATT: That's the highlight of the ride.

00:13:26.610 --> 00:13:27.050 position:63%
Let's do it.

00:13:27.090 --> 00:13:27.750 position:63%
NICK: Yeah, yeah.

00:13:27.790 --> 00:13:31.290 position:63%
[track clattering]

00:13:31.330 --> 00:13:31.730 position:63%
[riders screaming]

00:13:31.770 --> 00:13:32.370 position:63%
Woo. So here comes

00:13:32.410 --> 00:13:33.790 position:63%
MATT: So this is it here, right?

00:13:33.830 --> 00:13:34.850 position:63%
[track clattering]

00:13:34.890 --> 00:13:35.290 position:63%
[riders screaming]

00:13:35.330 --> 00:13:36.030 position:63%
That's smooth.

00:13:36.070 --> 00:13:37.760 position:63%
NICK: Very smooth transition in and out.

00:13:38.130 --> 00:13:39.100 position:63%
MATT: Ugh, that was deliberate.

00:13:39.410 --> 00:13:39.710 position:63%
NICK: Yes.

00:13:39.710 --> 00:13:40.510 position:63%
[laughs]

00:13:40.550 --> 00:13:44.590 position:63%
[track clattering]

00:13:44.630 --> 00:13:46.390 position:63%
[riders screaming]

00:13:46.430 --> 00:13:48.440 position:63%
Now, this bit is intentionally
a bit rough.

00:13:48.770 --> 00:13:53.490 position:63%
Right. Got it.
But within your planned tolerances?

00:13:53.530 --> 00:13:54.630 position:63%
- Yeah. Oh, absolutely.
- Yeah.

00:13:54.670 --> 00:13:55.640 position:63%
NICK: All within spec.

00:13:55.730 --> 00:13:56.570 position:63%
[track clattering]
[riders screaming]

00:13:56.610 --> 00:13:58.520 position:63%
MATT: So, can we see the straight section?

00:13:58.810 --> 00:14:01.350 position:63%
NICK: It's directly beneath
that curve right there.

00:14:01.390 --> 00:14:01.690 position:63%
MATT: Under there. Right.

00:14:01.450 --> 00:14:03.810 position:63%
'Cause that's the top of the
360-degree curve.

00:14:03.850 --> 00:14:12.210 position:63%
[track clattering]

00:14:12.250 --> 00:14:13.090 position:63%
[riders screaming]

00:14:13.130 --> 00:14:13.630 position:63%
- All right.
- Okay.

00:14:13.670 --> 00:14:16.040 position:63%
NICK: So watch as the train goes out and

00:14:18.050 --> 00:14:18.430 position:63%
back in.

00:14:18.470 --> 00:14:20.460 position:63%
- That wasn't there.
- That was brand new.

00:14:20.990 --> 00:14:24.410 position:63%
That's... I wouldn't have noticed
that, but it was good.

00:14:24.450 --> 00:14:27.470 position:63%
[track clattering]

00:14:27.510 --> 00:14:28.110 position:63%
MATT: That was really nice.

00:14:28.150 --> 00:14:28.950 position:63%
I could feel,

00:14:30.050 --> 00:14:33.020 position:63%
I could feel the movement, but without
feeling like I was being thrown around.

00:14:33.090 --> 00:14:34.810 position:63%
NICK: Yeah, yeah. Well,
and that's the key.

00:14:34.850 --> 00:14:36.510 position:63%
You feel the movement,

00:14:36.550 --> 00:14:40.140 position:63%
but from an actual acceleration
perspective, your body is not,

00:14:40.490 --> 00:14:43.960 position:63%
you know, doesn't feel any
excessive G-forces or lack thereof.

00:14:44.130 --> 00:14:45.300 position:63%
MATT: You could sell that as a feature.

00:14:45.370 --> 00:14:47.090 position:63%
I feel like that's innovation.

00:14:47.130 --> 00:14:48.330 position:63%
- Ooh.
- I felt that.

00:14:48.370 --> 00:14:49.530 position:63%
[laughs]

00:14:49.570 --> 00:14:51.890 position:63%
MATT: Given it's no longer just
straight lines and circles,

00:14:51.930 --> 00:14:55.770 position:63%
if you're wondering what the theoretical
geometry that Nick actually ends up with,

00:14:55.810 --> 00:14:59.700 position:63%
integrating back from the forces that
they want the passengers to experience,

00:14:59.990 --> 00:15:01.510 position:63%
it's something called an Euler curve.

00:15:01.550 --> 00:15:03.910 position:63%
You can look that up if you
wanna learn more about it.

00:15:03.950 --> 00:15:09.350 position:63%
And speaking about learning things, this
video was made due to the fantastic help

00:15:09.390 --> 00:15:13.890 position:63%
of many people at Disney, and they
suggested I put it out during STEAM week.

00:15:13.930 --> 00:15:16.420 position:63%
Because to make everything
Disney does possible,

00:15:16.610 --> 00:15:21.960 position:63%
you need to learn all of science,
technology, engineering, arts, and maths.

00:15:22.170 --> 00:15:23.870 position:63%
You might be wondering,
how do you become Nick?

00:15:23.910 --> 00:15:24.870 position:63%
[laughs]

00:15:24.910 --> 00:15:26.020 position:63%
Nick, how did you become Nick?

00:15:26.390 --> 00:15:29.770 position:63%
So I grew up about 15 minutes
from a major theme park,

00:15:29.810 --> 00:15:33.010 position:63%
about 1,000 miles north of here,
and it was in my blood as a kid.

00:15:33.050 --> 00:15:35.780 position:63%
NICK: It was what I wanted to
do from a really early age.

00:15:35.830 --> 00:15:36.130 position:63%
MATT: Yeah.

00:15:35.910 --> 00:15:40.470 position:63%
And I learned from an early age that
to do that, to be a part of this industry,

00:15:40.510 --> 00:15:43.920 position:63%
NICK: I needed an engineering degree, or at least to do the type of design I wanted.

00:15:44.270 --> 00:15:46.100 position:63%
So I went to the University of Cincinnati.

00:15:46.170 --> 00:15:49.000 position:63%
I got a bachelor's degree and a master's
degree in mechanical engineering,

00:15:49.330 --> 00:15:53.850 position:63%
and then I started as an intern here at
Walt Disney World in the engineering team.

00:15:53.890 --> 00:15:54.510 position:63%
- Amazing.
- Yeah.

00:15:54.550 --> 00:15:58.030 position:63%
- So maths at high school.
- A lot of math, yeah.

00:15:58.070 --> 00:15:59.110 position:63%
- Write that down.
- Yes.

00:15:59.150 --> 00:15:59.650 position:63%
[laughs]

00:15:59.690 --> 00:16:01.760 position:63%
NICK: Calc one, you know, be prepared.

00:16:01.810 --> 00:16:04.810 position:63%
And we were talking off camera about
a ride, and you were like, "Well,

00:16:04.850 --> 00:16:07.590 position:63%
- we just had to invent a new mechanism-"
- Yeah.

00:16:07.630 --> 00:16:08.690 position:63%
- "to do that."
- Yep.

00:16:08.730 --> 00:16:10.270 position:63%
- But you've got the in-house-
- We do

00:16:10.310 --> 00:16:11.170 position:63%
MATT: engineering know-how.

00:16:11.210 --> 00:16:15.010 position:63%
We do. Disney has, obviously,
with Walt Disney Imagineering and even the

00:16:15.050 --> 00:16:18.890 position:63%
local engineers here, there's a lot of
engineering brainpower that I'm sure a lot

00:16:18.930 --> 00:16:22.430 position:63%
NICK: of the theme park companies
don't have the same exposure to.

00:16:22.470 --> 00:16:25.210 position:63%
But that just allows us to
push the envelope even further,

00:16:25.250 --> 00:16:27.430 position:63%
to develop these new experiences,
these new technologies-

00:16:27.470 --> 00:16:28.010 position:63%
MATT: Right.

00:16:28.050 --> 00:16:30.110 position:63%
NICK: and create things that
have never been done before.

00:16:30.150 --> 00:16:47.920 position:63%
[gentle music]

00:16:49.610 --> 00:16:50.410 position:63%
[track clattering]

